= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Separate the variables in this <first-order ordinary differential equation>:
$$
\frac{y}{y^2+2}\,dy=\frac{x}{x^2-3}\,dx.
$$
Integration gives $\tfrac12\ln(y^2+2)=\tfrac12\ln|x^2-3|+C$, so $y^2+2=K|x^2-3|$. On the interval containing the prescribed point $x=3$, we have $x^2-3>0$. The <initial condition> $y(3)=4$ therefore gives $18=6K$, hence $K=3$. Choosing the positive <square root> to match that <initial condition> yields
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sqrt{3x^2-11}.}
$$
The maximal interval containing $3$ on which this solves the explicit <ordinary differential equation> is $\boxed{x>\sqrt{11/3}}$. At the left endpoint $y=0$, the original denominator vanishes and the <derivative> becomes unbounded. Directly, $y'=3x/y$ and $x(2+y^2)/[y(x^2-3)]=3x/y$, verifying the equation throughout this interval.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Recognize the left-hand side as a <product rule> <derivative>, and use $\tfrac12\sin2x=\sin x\cos x$:
$$
\frac{d}{dx}(y\sin x)=\sin x\cos x,\qquad y\sin x=\frac12\sin^2x+C.
$$
At $x=\pi/6$, both $y\sin x$ and $\tfrac12\sin^2x$ equal $1/8$, so $C=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac12\sin x.}
$$
Dividing by $\sin x$ first obtains this solution on $(0,\pi)$, the interval containing the <initial condition>. The explicit expression is smooth on the entire real line and satisfies the original, undivided <differential equation> even where $\sin x=0$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For this <homogeneous first-order differential equation>, set $v=y/x$, with $x\ne0$. The <product rule> gives $y'=v+xv'$, and substitution simplifies the equation to
$$
(1+2ve^v)xv'=2(1-v^2e^v).
$$
The useful further substitution is $w=e^{-v}-v^2$. Its <chain rule> <derivative> satisfies
$$
x\frac{dw}{dx}=-(e^{-v}+2v)xv'=-2(e^{-v}-v^2)=-2w.
$$
Thus $(x^2w)'=0$. Replacing $v$ and $w$ gives the <implicit solution>
$$
\boxed{x^2e^{-y/x}-y^2=C,\qquad x\ne0.}
$$
No division by $w$ was needed, so the level $C=0$ is included. A direct verification also identifies the <integrating factor for a differential one-form>:
$$
d\bigl(x^2e^{-y/x}-y^2\bigr)=e^{-y/x}(2x+y)\,dx-(xe^{-y/x}+2y)\,dy.
$$
Setting this <exact differential> to zero recovers precisely the original equation after multiplication by the nonzero factor $e^{y/x}$. The coefficient $1+2ve^v$ never vanishes for real $v$, since $ve^v\geq-1/e$ implies $1+2ve^v\geq1-2/e>0$. Hence these <level sets> give local <function graphs> $y(x)$ everywhere with $x\ne0$.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <generalized Leibniz rule> follows by <mathematical induction>. The case $n=0$ is the identity $uv=uv$. Suppose the formula holds for $n$ and differentiate every term using the <product rule>:
$$
(uv)^{(n+1)}=\sum_{r=0}^n\binom nr\left[u^{(r+1)}v^{(n-r)}+u^{(r)}v^{(n-r+1)}\right].
$$
For an interior index $r$, the coefficient of $u^{(r)}v^{(n+1-r)}$ becomes $\binom n{r-1}+\binom nr=\binom{n+1}r$ by <Pascal's identity>. The two endpoint coefficients are one. This proves
$$
\boxed{(uv)^{(n)}=\sum_{r=0}^n\binom nr u^{(r)}v^{(n-r)},\qquad\binom nr=\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}.}
$$
Only finitely many <derivatives> are involved; it suffices that both <functions> are $n$ times differentiable.

For $u=x(x+1)$ and $v=e^{2x}$, the <derivatives> of $u$ beyond order two vanish. The three surviving terms of the <generalized Leibniz rule> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d^n}{dx^n}\bigl[x(x+1)e^{2x}\bigr]
&=e^{2x}\left[2^n(x^2+x)+n2^{n-1}(2x+1)+n(n-1)2^{n-2}\right]\\
&=\boxed{2^ne^{2x}\left[x^2+(n+1)x+\frac{n(n+1)}4\right].}
\end{aligned}
$$
The simplified expression works for every integer $n\geq0$, including the two cases where one or more of the three terms are absent.

For $u=x^2$ and $v=\sin x$, use $d^m(\sin x)/dx^m=\sin(x+m\pi/2)$. Again only three terms survive. With $T=x+n\pi/2$, they are
$$
\frac{d^n}{dx^n}(x^2\sin x)=x^2\sin T+2nx\sin(T-\pi/2)+n(n-1)\sin(T-\pi).
$$
Since $\sin(T-\pi/2)=-\cos T$ and $\sin(T-\pi)=-\sin T$, this becomes
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^n}{dx^n}(x^2\sin x)=\bigl[x^2-n(n-1)\bigr]\sin\left(x+\frac{n\pi}2\right)-2nx\cos\left(x+\frac{n\pi}2\right).}
$$
The factors $n$ and $n(n-1)$ remove the nonexistent terms when $n=0$ or $1$.

