= Solution
{parent=a}

The conversion from <cylindrical coordinates> to <Cartesian coordinates> is
$$
\boxed{x=\rho\cos\phi,\qquad y=\rho\sin\phi,\qquad z=z.}
$$
Here $\rho\geq0$ is the distance from the $z$ axis and $\phi$ is measured around that axis. A full turn $\phi\mapsto\phi+2\pi$ describes the same point.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the <circular cylinder>, $\rho=R$ is constant, so the given <line element> reduces to $ds^2=R^2d\phi^2+dz^2$. Unroll the <circular cylinder> with $u=R\phi$. The <line element> is then $ds^2=du^2+dz^2$, so the usual <Euclidean geometry> formula measures <arc length> in the $(u,z)$ <plane>.

The endpoint has infinitely many lifts $(R(\phi_1+2\pi k),z_1)$, with $k\in\mathbb Z$. For each lift, the <straight line> segment is shortest; its <length> is $\sqrt{R^2(\phi_1+2\pi k)^2+z_1^2}$. Comparing the lifts gives the unrestricted <shortest path on a circular cylinder>:
$$
\boxed{L_{\min}=\min_{k\in\mathbb Z}\sqrt{R^2(\phi_1+2\pi k)^2+z_1^2}.}
$$
If $\delta$ is a minimizing angular increment, a shortest <geodesic> is parametrized by $(\rho,\phi,z)=(R,\delta t,z_1t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. This is a <helix> when both increments are nonzero, and a circular arc or vertical generator in the limiting cases. A nonminimal lift also gives a <geodesic>, but it is only shortest within its fixed winding class.

For $0\leq\phi_1\leq2\pi$, put $m=\min(\phi_1,2\pi-\phi_1)$. Then $\boxed{L_{\min}=\sqrt{R^2m^2+z_1^2}}$. At $\phi_1=\pi$, both winding directions give distinct shortest paths of equal <length>.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $R=1$ and $z_1=0$, the <shortest path on a circular cylinder> has <length>
$$
\boxed{L_0(\phi_1)=\begin{cases}\phi_1,&0\leq\phi_1\leq\pi,\\2\pi-\phi_1,&\pi\leq\phi_1\leq2\pi.\end{cases}}
$$
Its graph is triangular, with endpoint values zero and maximum $\pi$ at $\phi_1=\pi$. In particular, increasing the angle to a full turn brings the endpoint back to the starting point.

For $R=1$ and $z_1=1$, the corresponding <length> is
$$
\boxed{L_1(\phi_1)=\sqrt{1+\min(\phi_1,2\pi-\phi_1)^2}.}
$$
This graph has endpoint values one, a maximum $\sqrt{1+\pi^2}$ at $\pi$, and symmetry about $\pi$. Each half is smooth, with horizontal slope at the outer endpoint; at $\pi$ the two halves meet at a corner, because the minimizing winding direction changes.

\Image[paper-2-q3-cylinder-shortest-paths.png]
{title=Unrolling a cylinder and choosing the shorter angular route}
{description=Original diagram. Left: two lifts of an endpoint at angular position $3\pi/2$ and height one; the negative angular increment $-\pi/2$ gives the shorter segment. Right: both requested shortest-length graphs for unit radius, with height differences zero and one.}

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

At an ordinary point, an order-$n$ <linear ordinary differential equation> has $n$ arbitrary constants in its general solution. Determining them uniquely requires $n$ independent, consistent scalar conditions. Prescribing $y,y',\ldots,y^{(n-1)}$ at one ordinary point gives uniqueness under the standard continuous-coefficient assumptions. Conditions at different endpoints need an additional nonsingularity check: for example, $y''+y=0$, $y(0)=y(\pi)=0$ leaves every multiple of $\sin x$ possible. Thus counting <boundary conditions> alone does not guarantee uniqueness for a <boundary value problem>.

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic equation> is $m^2+3m+2=(m+1)(m+2)=0$, giving the <complementary function> $C_1e^{-x}+C_2e^{-2x}$. For the forcing term $x$, a linear <particular solution> $ax+b$ gives $2ax+3a+2b=x$, hence $a=1/2$, $b=-3/4$.

The forcing $-e^{-x}$ gives <resonance in a differential equation>, since $e^{-x}$ already appears in the <complementary function>. Try $Kxe^{-x}$. Direct differentiation gives
$$
\left(\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+3\frac d{dx}+2\right)(Kxe^{-x})=Ke^{-x},
$$
so $K=-1$. Combining the two <particular solutions> gives
$$
\boxed{y=(C_1-x)e^{-x}+C_2e^{-2x}+\frac x2-\frac34.}
$$
The factor $x$ in the <resonant exponential particular solution> is required because $-1$ is already a root of the <characteristic equation>.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Now the <characteristic equation> is $(m+1)^2=0$, with a <repeated root>, so the <complementary function> is $(C_1+C_2x)e^{-x}$. For the polynomial forcing, substitute $ax^2+bx+c$ to obtain
$$
ax^2+(4a+b)x+(2a+2b+c)=x^2.
$$
Thus $a=1$, $b=-4$, $c=6$. For the forcing $2e^{-x}$, both $e^{-x}$ and $xe^{-x}$ already occur in the <complementary function>; the <resonant exponential particular solution> therefore starts with $Kx^2e^{-x}$. The identity
$$
\left(\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+2\frac d{dx}+1\right)(e^{-x}v)=e^{-x}v''
$$
gives $K=1$. The resulting general solution is
$$
\boxed{y=(C_1+C_2x+x^2)e^{-x}+x^2-4x+6.}
$$

