Parametrize the <straight line> by $r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, with $r'(t)=(1,1,1)$. Substituting into the <vector field> and taking the <dot product> gives
$$
v(r(t))\cdot r'(t)=(t+1)e^t+5t^4.
$$
Hence the direct <line integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_Cv\cdot dr=\int_0^1\bigl[(t+1)e^t+5t^4\bigr]\,dt=\left[te^t+t^5\right]_0^1=e+1.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the curved path $r(t)=(t,t^2,t^3)$, $r'(t)=(1,2t,3t^2)$. The <vector field> along this <parametric curve> is
$$
v(r(t))=(t^2e^t,\ e^t+2t^{11},\ 3t^{10}).
$$
Its <dot product> with the tangent is $(t^2+2t)e^t+13t^{12}$. Therefore the direct <line integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_Cv\cdot dr=\int_0^1\bigl[(t^2+2t)e^t+13t^{12}\bigr]\,dt=\left[t^2e^t+t^{13}\right]_0^1=e+1.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Compute the <curl> in <Cartesian coordinates>:
$$
\nabla\times v=\left(\partial_y(3y^2z^2)-\partial_z(e^x+2yz^3),\ \partial_z(e^xy)-\partial_x(3y^2z^2),\ \partial_x(e^x+2yz^3)-\partial_y(e^xy)\right).
$$
The three components are $6yz^2-6yz^2$, $0-0$, and $e^x-e^x$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times v=(0,0,0).}
$$

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Integrate $f_x=ye^x$ with respect to $x$, obtaining $f=ye^x+g(y,z)$. Matching $f_y=e^x+2yz^3$ gives $g_y=2yz^3$, so $g=y^2z^3+h(z)$. Finally, $f_z=3y^2z^2+h'(z)$ must equal the third component, so $h'=0$. Thus a <scalar potential> is
$$
\boxed{f(x,y,z)=ye^x+y^2z^3+C,\qquad v=\nabla f.}
$$
The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> now gives $\int_Cv\cdot dr=f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0)=e+1$ for either path, confirming both direct computations. Since the <curl of a gradient> is zero for this smooth <function>, it also confirms the result in part (b). The potential is defined throughout $\mathbb R^3$, so no domain obstruction to <path independence> occurs.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use a <Lagrange multiplier> to maximize $V=\pi r^2h$ subject to $2\pi r(r+h)=A$, with $r,h>0$. The equations $\nabla V=\lambda\nabla A$ are
$$
2\pi rh=\lambda(4\pi r+2\pi h),\qquad\pi r^2=2\lambda\pi r.
$$
The second equation gives $\lambda=r/2$. Substituting into the first gives $2rh=r(2r+h)$, hence $h=2r$. The <constraint> then gives $A=6\pi r^2$, so
$$
\boxed{r=\sqrt{\frac A{6\pi}},\qquad h=2\sqrt{\frac A{6\pi}},\qquad V_{\max}=2\pi\left(\frac A{6\pi}\right)^{3/2}=\frac13\sqrt{\frac{A^3}{6\pi}}.}
$$
This is the same aspect ratio as the <minimum-area closed cylinder at fixed volume>. To confirm maximality directly, eliminate $h$: $V(r)=Ar/2-\pi r^3$ on $0<r<\sqrt{A/(2\pi)}$. Its only stationary point is the value above, its second <derivative> there is negative, and the <volume> tends to zero at either end of the interval.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Maximize the total <surface area> $A=2\pi r(r+h)$ with the <constraint> $g=r^2+h^2/4=1$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
4\pi r+2\pi h=2\lambda r,\qquad2\pi r=\lambda h/2.
$$
From the second, $\lambda=4\pi r/h$. Substitute into the first and multiply by $h/(2\pi)$ to obtain $2rh+h^2=4r^2$. Thus the aspect ratio $q=h/r$ satisfies $q^2+2q-4=0$. Since $q>0$,
$$
\boxed{\frac hr=\sqrt5-1.}
$$
This is the <maximum-area cylinder inscribed in a sphere>. The dimensions and area, although not needed to specify the ratio, are
$$
r^2=\frac{5+\sqrt5}{10},\qquad h^2=\frac{2(5-\sqrt5)}5,\qquad A_{\max}=\pi(1+\sqrt5).
$$
There is only one positive stationary ratio. At the degenerate boundaries $r=0$ and $h=0$, the limiting areas are zero and $2\pi$, both below this value, confirming the maximum.

