= Solution
{parent=a}

The <region of integration> is $0\leq y\leq1$, $y\leq x\leq2-y$. It is the closed <triangle> with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, and $(1,1)$, bounded by $y=0$, $x=y$, and $x=2-y$. Each horizontal slice runs from the rising left edge to the falling right edge.

\Image[paper-2-q8-triangular-region.png]
{title=Triangular integration region and a horizontal integration slice}
{description=Original diagram of the region $0\leq y\leq1$, $y\leq x\leq2-y$, with its three vertices, boundary equations, and an example slice at fixed $y$.}

Integrate with respect to $x$ first:
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_0^1\left[\frac23x^3+yx\right]_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\,dy\\
&=\int_0^1\left[\frac{16}3-6y+2y^2-\frac43y^3\right]dy\\
&=\frac{16}3-3+\frac23-\frac13=\boxed{\frac83}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <triangle> has area one, but the nonconstant integrand makes the <double integral> a weighted area.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <spherical polar coordinates> <volume element> $dV=r^2\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta\,d\phi$. Since $1+\cos\theta\geq0$ throughout $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$, the solid has radial range $0\leq r\leq1+\cos\theta$ and full azimuthal range $0\leq\phi<2\pi$. Its <volume> is
$$
V=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\pi\int_0^{1+\cos\theta}r^2\sin\theta\,dr\,d\theta\,d\phi=\frac{2\pi}3\int_0^\pi(1+\cos\theta)^3\sin\theta\,d\theta.
$$
Put $u=1+\cos\theta$, so $du=-\sin\theta\,d\theta$ and the endpoints become $2$ and $0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{V=\frac{2\pi}3\int_0^2u^3\,du=\frac{8\pi}3.}
$$
The polar equation describes the entire three-dimensional surface, so the <spherical polar coordinates> <volume element>, rather than a two-dimensional polar area element, is required.

