= Solution
{parent=i}

Solve the two algebraic <constraints> for the squared coordinates:
$$
x^2=\frac{z^2+u^2}{3},\qquad y^2=\frac{z^2-2u^2}{3}.
$$
On any nonsingular real branch, <implicit differentiation> at fixed $u$ gives $(\partial x/\partial z)_u=z/(3x)$ and $(\partial y/\partial z)_u=z/(3y)$. Differentiating $z^2=2x^2+y^2$ with the other original coordinate fixed gives $(\partial z/\partial x)_y=2x/z$ and $(\partial z/\partial y)_x=y/z$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial z}\right)_u\left(\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\right)_y+\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial z}\right)_u\left(\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right)_x=\frac z{3x}\frac{2x}z+\frac z{3y}\frac yz=\frac23+\frac13=1.}
$$
This is the <multivariable chain rule> applied to $z(x(z,u),y(z,u))=z$. These formulas assume the relevant denominators are nonzero and the chosen variables are valid local coordinates.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At fixed $z$, differentiating $2x^2+y^2=z^2$ gives $(\partial x/\partial y)_z=-y/(2x)$. Also, $x^2=(z^2+u^2)/3$ gives $(\partial x/\partial u)_z=u/(3x)$. At fixed $y$, eliminate $x^2$ to write $u^2=(z^2-3y^2)/2$, giving $(\partial u/\partial z)_y=z/(2u)$. Finally, the previous part gave $(\partial y/\partial z)_u=z/(3y)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right)_z\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial z}\right)_u+\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial u}\right)_z\left(\frac{\partial u}{\partial z}\right)_y=-\frac y{2x}\frac z{3y}+\frac u{3x}\frac z{2u}=0.}
$$
The held-fixed subscripts matter: substituting a reciprocal <partial derivative> while silently changing what is held fixed would not preserve these cancellations.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate both algebraic <constraints> at fixed $y$ or fixed $x$:
$$
z_x=\frac{2x}z,\quad z_y=\frac yz,\qquad u_x=\frac xu,\quad u_y=-\frac yu.
$$
The <multivariable chain rule> for $\phi(x,y)=\psi(z,u)$ therefore gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x}\right)_y=\frac{2x}z\left(\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial z}\right)_u+\frac xu\left(\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial u}\right)_z,\qquad\left(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial y}\right)_x=\frac yz\left(\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial z}\right)_u-\frac yu\left(\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial u}\right)_z.
$$
Divide the first identity by $x$ and the second by $y$, and add. The $\psi_u$ terms cancel, leaving
$$
\boxed{\frac1x\left(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial x}\right)_y+\frac1y\left(\frac{\partial\phi}{\partial y}\right)_x=\frac3z\left(\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial z}\right)_u.}
$$
The <Jacobian determinant> of $(x,y)\mapsto(z,u)$ is $-3xy/(zu)$, so the calculation is valid on real branches with $xyzu\ne0$. This explicitly supplies the local independence assumption underlying the change of coordinates.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

If $P\,dx+Q\,dy=dF$ for a twice continuously differentiable potential $F$, then $P=F_x$ and $Q=F_y$. Equality of <mixed partial derivatives> gives the necessary condition for an <exact differential>:
$$
\boxed{P_y=Q_x.}
$$
This condition alone does not ensure global exactness on a domain with holes.

For the <Helmholtz free energy> $A=U-TS$, use the stated identity for <internal energy> and the <product rule>:
$$
dA=dU-T\,dS-S\,dT=-S\,dT-p\,dV.
$$
Thus $A_T|_V=-S$ and $A_V|_T=-p$. Equality of <mixed partial derivatives> yields the <Maxwell relation>
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T=\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V.}
$$
The <temperature> and <volume (thermodynamics)> are the independent variables here, with composition and amount of gas fixed.

Next express $S=S(T,V)$ and substitute its <total differential> into $dU=T\,dS-p\,dV$:
$$
dU=T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial T}\right)_VdT+\left[T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T-p\right]dV.
$$
Matching the coefficient of $dV$ gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_T=T\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial V}\right)_T-p=T\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V-p.}
$$
This is the <thermodynamic internal pressure> identity: an <equation of state> determines how <internal energy> changes under an isothermal change of <volume (thermodynamics)>.

For the <Dieterici equation>, set $c=an/(VR)$ while differentiating at fixed $V$. Then
$$
p=\frac{nR}{V-nb}Te^{-c/T},\qquad\left(\frac{\partial p}{\partial T}\right)_V=\frac{nR}{V-nb}e^{-c/T}\left(1+\frac cT\right).
$$
Substitute into the <thermodynamic internal pressure> identity. The term proportional to $T$ cancels $p$, leaving
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial V}\right)_T=\frac{an^2}{V(V-nb)}\exp\left(-\frac{an}{VRT}\right).}
$$
The physical gas domain has $T>0$ and $V>nb$.

For the <ideal gas> instead, $p=nRT/V$ gives $T(\partial p/\partial T)_V-p=0$. Therefore $U$ is independent of <volume (thermodynamics)> at fixed amount of gas. Integrating the remaining condition $(\partial U/\partial T)_V=C_V$, with constant <heat capacity at constant volume>, gives
$$
\boxed{U(T,V)=C_VT+U_0,}
$$
where $U_0$ is independent of both $T$ and $V$ and fixes the reference for <internal energy>.

= 11
