= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With replacement, successive draws are <independent>, with red <probability> $2/7$ and green <probability> $5/7$. The first red on draw $n$ requires $n-1$ greens followed by one red. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N=n)=\left(\frac57\right)^{n-1}\frac27,\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
This is a <geometric distribution>. Its total <probability> is one because the <geometric series> sums to $(2/7)/(1-5/7)=1$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

There are two red counters among seven equally likely first draws, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E_1)=\frac27.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the <law of total probability> according to the first draw. Without replacement, the second-draw red <probability> is $1/6$ after a red and $2/6$ after a green. Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E_2)=\frac27\frac16+\frac57\frac26=\frac27.}
$$
Equivalently, each counter has the same <probability> of occupying the second position as the first. Equal marginal <probabilities> do not make the two draw events <independent>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

After a red first draw, one red remains among six counters. The multiplication rule for <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E_1\cap E_2)=\mathbb P(E_1)\mathbb P(E_2\mid E_1)=\frac27\frac16=\frac1{21}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

By <inclusion-exclusion>, subtract the intersection once to correct the double counting:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E_1\cup E_2)=\mathbb P(E_1)+\mathbb P(E_2)-\mathbb P(E_1\cap E_2)=\frac27+\frac27-\frac1{21}=\frac{11}{21}.}
$$
As a check, the complementary event is two greens, of <probability> $(5/7)(4/6)=10/21$.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The definition of <conditional probability> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E_1\mid E_2)=\frac{\mathbb P(E_1\cap E_2)}{\mathbb P(E_2)}=\frac{1/21}{2/7}=\frac16.}
$$
Conditioning on a red in the second position leaves one red among the six counters that could occupy the first position, giving the same result directly.

