= Solution
{parent=i}

Using the standard <sine> <limit> $\sin t/t\to1$, with angles in radians,
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin2x}{x}=2\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\sin2x}{2x}=2.}
$$
Thus the two-sided <limit> exists and is finite.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Although $\cos(1/x)$ oscillates without a <limit>, its magnitude is at most one. Therefore $|x\cos(1/x)|\leq|x|\to0$. The <squeeze theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}x\cos(1/x)=0.}
$$
The shrinking factor $x$ suppresses the oscillation from either side of zero.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <derivative> of the <exponential function> at zero gives $e^{-x}=1-x+O(x^2)$. Hence $1-e^{-x}=x+O(x^2)$, and
$$
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}\frac{x}{1-e^{-x}}=1.}
$$
The denominator is nonzero for every real $x\ne0$, so this is a two-sided finite <limit>.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <series> $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}u_n$ is a <convergent series> if its <partial sums> $S_N=\sum_{n=1}^Nu_n$ have a finite <limit> $S$ as $N\to\infty$. The number $S$ is the sum of the <series>. Explicitly, for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is an integer $N_0$ such that $|S_N-S|<\varepsilon$ whenever $N\geq N_0$. The condition $u_n\to0$ alone is insufficient: the <harmonic series> has terms tending to zero but diverges.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <series> $\sum u_n$ has <absolute convergence> if $\sum|u_n|$ is a <convergent series>, meaning its nonnegative <partial sums> have a finite <limit>. <Absolute convergence> implies convergence of the original real or complex <series>. The converse fails: the <alternating harmonic series> converges, while its absolute-value <series> is the divergent <harmonic series>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The summands $u_n=n^4/2^n$ are positive. Apply the <ratio test>:
$$
\frac{u_{n+1}}{u_n}=\frac{(n+1)^4}{2n^4}=\frac12\left(1+\frac1n\right)^4\longrightarrow\frac12<1.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n^4}{2^n}\text{ converges absolutely}.}
$$
For a direct comparison interpretation, the ratio is eventually at most any fixed number between $1/2$ and $1$, such as $3/4$. The tail is then bounded by a convergent <geometric series>. Thus the exponential denominator wins over the fourth-power numerator strongly enough to make the whole <series> summable.
